    {
      "locator": "page 1",
      "text": " \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nSmoke Experiment \naka \nCharacterization of Smoke Particulate  \nfor Spacecraft Fire Detection \n \nEngineering Report \n \nCharacterization of Ignitor Wire Candidates \n \n \n \n \n \nSubmitted by: \nZIN Technologies, Inc \nLen Miller \nApril 2002 \n \n"
    },
    {
      "locator": "page 2",
      "text": "SMOKE Experiment \nIgnitor WIre Characterization Study \nApril 2002 \nPurpose: \nThe purpose of this report is to document the results of a study conducted in search of the \nideal type of wire to be used for the hot wire ignitors for the Smoke Experiment. \n \nObjective: \nThe objective of this study was to find a wire with a consistent, linear resistance versus \ntemperature characteristic and with a “significant” slope. \n \nScope: \nThis study was conducted in the Phase A Laboratory (Bldg 77, Room 154A) on \ncommercially available wire.  The ignitor manufacturing technique and the pyrolization \nof samples is beyond the scope of this study. \n \nProcedure: \nEach type of wire was mounted on a test fixture.  The test fixture was then placed into a \nThermCraft laboratory oven.  The oven was programmed to ramp up to a set point, hold \nthe set point for fifteen minutes, and then ramp to the next set point until the final \ntemperature of approximately 800 oC was attained.  (See oven cycle graph in Appendix \nC.)  Resistance readings were taken near the end of  each “dwell” period.  Several of the \nwire types were subjected to additional tests to examine the effects of the previous \nthermal cycle(s). \n \nThe test fixture held approximately thirty-four inches (0.86 meters) of wire. \n \nA stainless steel sheathed type-k thermocouple (Omega Part No. KQSS-116U-24) was \nplaced inside the oven in close proximity to the wire under test.   \n \nLeads of high temperature furnace wire (#18 AWG) were brought out of the oven to \nenable the test conductor to measure the resistance of the wire under test.   \nThe resistance of the leads and the terminations was characterized by subjection the \nfixture and leads to a thermal cycle with a copper bus bar shorting the terminals of the \nfixture. \n \nThe fixture resistance was subtracted from the total resistance reading for each test point \nto determine the net resistance of the wire under test. \n \nThe results were recorded in an Excel Workbook.  Several different data plots were \nprepared and studied. \n"
    },
    {
      "locator": "page 3",
      "text": "SMOKE Experiment \nIgnitor WIre Characterization Study \nApril 2002 \n \nSummary and Conclusions: \nThis study examined the characteristics of eight types of wire: anodized mild steel, pure \niron, Alomega (similar to Alumel), -Stainless steel, pure platinum, platinum-rhodium, \ntungster-5%rhenium, and tungsten-26%rhenium.  Traditional heating wires, Nichrome \nand Kanthal were also included for comparison. \n \nPure platinum has close-to-ideal characteristics.  However the cost of the material may be \nprohibitive. \n \nAlomega and stainless steel ran a close second. \n \nMild steel, iron and tungsten-composites oxidized in the oven and their characteristics \nchanged significantly from one thermal cycle to the next. \n \nNichrome and Kanthal have too flat a slope to meet the objectives. \n \nFurther testing will be conducted to determine the optimum wire type and wire diameter.  \nRepresentative test samples will by pyrolized using various ignitor configurations.  The \nquality and quantity of the smoke will be visually evaluated. \n \nTest Results: \nThe tests were conducted in the Phase A Lab from February 6, 2002 through March 20 \n2002, by Len Miller and Nathan Funk of Zin Technologies, Inc. \n \nThe individual results for each test were tabulated and plotted.  For those wires subjected \nto more than one thermal cycle, the comparative results were plotted.  Finally, an overall \nplot of the combined results was prepared.   \n \nThe entire Excel Workbook, with tabulated test results and plots thereof, is included in  \nAppendix A of this report.  \n \nPhotographs of the laboratory apparatus are in Appendix B and a graph of the thermal \ncycle is in Appendix C. \n \n"
    },
